#. §0: `apply Or.elim nonzero`
#: Game/Levels/OptimizationArena/L02_PositiveSecondVariation.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Classify the quadratic minimum using its positive second variation.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: First and second partial derivatives; compact regions; elementary inequalities. Earlier levels of Optimization Arena.\n"
"\n"
"The two diagonal second partials of x²+y² are 2. To justify strict positivity of its second variation, a nonzero displacement has at least one nonzero coordinate. §0 gives the two alternatives; introduce the nonzero coordinate in each branch. Combine a strictly positive square with the other nonnegative square. A Hessian calculation is accompanied by the needed sign justification.\n"
"\n"
"Worked example: For f=3x²+y² the Hessian is diag(6,2). On a nonzero x-axis displacement (h,0), its quadratic form 6h² is strictly positive.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. The hints guide the main steps."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/CurveNavigator/L06_BossTheHelix.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Combine endpoints, velocity, and speed for a spatial curve.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Vector algebra; single-variable differentiation and integration. Earlier levels of Curve Navigator.\n"
"\n"
"The helix adds uniform vertical motion to a circle. Assemble its initial point, velocity at any parameter, and squared speed. The familiar identity sin²t+cos²t=1 supplies the final calculation. Use the existing linear-arithmetic tool to combine that identity with the additional vertical contribution 1.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. Combine earlier methods and justify every hypothesis."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/GaussCitadel/L06_FinalBossThreeIntegralBridges.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Combine justified special cases of Green, Stokes, and Gauss.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Divergence; oriented surface flux; triple integrals; integral theorem special cases. Earlier levels of Gauss Citadel.\n"
"\n"
"Combine three established integral bridges. Use the shear Green and Stokes identities on the positive square and upward patch. Use the diagonal Gauss family on the outward cube, noting that coefficients (1,1,1) reconstruct the radial field. Evaluate circulation 2 and flux 3 through their interior integrals, then add them. All three applications are justified proved special cases; the final boss introduces no new tactic.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. Combine earlier methods and justify every hypothesis."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/DoubleIntegralValley/L06_BossATriangularLamina.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Combine variable bounds, mass, and a first moment.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Single-variable integration; planar regions; mass and moments. Earlier levels of Double Integral Valley.\n"
"\n"
"For uniform density on 0≤y≤x≤1, derive the inner mass and moment integrals as x, x², and x²/2. Integrating these gives mass 1/2 and moments 1/3 and 1/6. Divide by the positive mass to obtain the full centroid (2/3,1/3). Reuse pointwise replacement and the power rule; the boss introduces no new proof syntax.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. Combine earlier methods and justify every hypothesis."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/SurfaceExpedition/L06_BossReverseThePatch.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Compare equal scalar area and opposite oriented flux.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Cross products; partial derivatives; double integration. Earlier levels of Surface Expedition.\n"
"\n"
"Interchanging the parameter order reverses the normal while preserving its length. Calculate both normals, the two fluxes, and the two scalar areas. Then compare the results. This distinguishes orientation-sensitive flux from orientation-independent area by a fully checked concrete example.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. Combine earlier methods and justify every hypothesis."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/OptimizationArena/L04_InteriorAndBoundary.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Compare global values over a closed disk, including its boundary.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: First and second partial derivatives; compact regions; elementary inequalities. Earlier levels of Optimization Arena.\n"
"\n"
"On the closed disk x²+y²≤1, compare values at every feasible point. The lower bound follows from the nonnegativity of the two squares; the upper bound is the region constraint. The origin attains 0, and every point of the unit-circle boundary attains 1. Bounds for arbitrary feasible points provide the global justification.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `vector_eq`
#. §1: `cross`
#. §2: `dot_cross_left`
#: Game/Levels/Vectors/L05_CrossingDirections.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Compute a cross product and verify perpendicularity.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Real arithmetic; vectors in three-dimensional space. Earlier levels of Vector Workshop.\n"
"\n"
"The right-hand rule fixes the order in a cross product. Compute its three components using §0 and the definition §1. For perpendicularity, apply the proved rule §2: any vector is perpendicular to its cross product with another vector.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/CoordinatePortal/L05_ANonpolarJacobian.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Compute a determinant for a linear coordinate transformation.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Multiple integrals; determinants; trigonometric identities. Earlier levels of Coordinate Portal.\n"
"\n"
"The map T(u,v)=(2u,-3v) has derivative matrix diag(2,-3). Derive its entries from the coordinate partials before taking the determinant. Name the signed determinant and reuse it for the absolute determinant. The negative sign records orientation reversal; area scaling uses its positive magnitude.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/VectorFieldObservatory/L03_Divergence.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Compute a field divergence using the appropriate coordinate derivatives.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Gradients, partial derivatives, and three-dimensional vectors. Earlier levels of Vector Field Observatory.\n"
"\n"
"Divergence adds only the matching-coordinate partials: ∂F₀/∂x₀, ∂F₁/∂x₁, and ∂F₂/∂x₂. For the radial field each is a coordinate derivative equal to 1. Choose those terms using the definition before calculating their sum.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `gradient_components`
#. §1: `partial_coordinate`
#: Game/Levels/GradientMountain/L01_AssembleAGradient.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Compute a gradient from coordinate partial derivatives.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Partial derivatives; unit directions; chain rule and matrices.\n"
"\n"
"The gradient collects the scalar partial derivatives in coordinate order. Open it using §0. The rule §1 says that differentiating a coordinate with respect to itself gives 1, while the other coordinate derivatives are zero.\n"
"\n"
"Worked example: For f(x,y,z)=3x, the three partials are 3, 0, and 0, assembled in that order into the gradient.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. The hints guide the main steps."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `apply vector_eq`
#. §1: `·`
#. §2: `norm_num`
#. §3: `apply vector_eq`
#. §4: `norm_num`
#: Game/Levels/Vectors/L02_AddingAndScaling.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Compute a linear combination coordinate by coordinate.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Real arithmetic; vectors in three-dimensional space. Earlier levels of Vector Workshop.\n"
"\n"
"Addition and scalar multiplication act on each coordinate. Use §0 to turn one vector equality into three real equalities. The three bullets §1 handle those goals in order. This proved rule lets you compare coordinates without learning how Lean implements finite functions. §2 checks concrete arithmetic; it does not choose the vector operation for you.\n"
"\n"
"Worked example: For u=(2,0,1) and v=(1,1,0), u+v=(3,1,1). After §3, check the three scalar sums under separate bullets.\n"
"\n"
"New tools unlocked here:\n"
"\n"
"§4: Check numerical coordinate arithmetic after expanding the relevant definitions.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. The hints guide the main steps."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `have`
#: Game/Levels/Vectors/L06_BossResolveAVector.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Compute a projection and show its remainder is orthogonal.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Real arithmetic; vectors in three-dimensional space. Earlier levels of Vector Workshop.\n"
"\n"
"Project v=(3,1,2) onto the nonzero direction n=(1,1,0). Its squared length is 2, so the projection coefficient (v·n)/(n·n) is 4/2. Name the resulting vector equality with §0, then reuse it when calculating the remainder. The challenge combines coordinate comparison, dot products, and projection; no new tactic is needed.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. Combine earlier methods and justify every hypothesis."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `directional_coordinate`
#. §1: `lengthSq u = 1`
#: Game/Levels/GradientMountain/L02_AUnitDirection.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Compute a total directional derivative with a unit-direction assumption.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Partial derivatives; unit directions; chain rule and matrices. Earlier levels of Gradient Mountain.\n"
"\n"
"A directional derivative applies the total derivative to the direction u. The general coordinate-projection rule §0 computes it without asking you to construct a continuous linear map. The hypothesis §1 specifies a unit direction, so the answer is a rate per unit distance. The underlying derivative identity also holds for nonunit vectors.\n"
"\n"
"Worked example: For the scalar field f(x,y,z)=x and unit direction (0,1,0), the directional rate is zero: moving in y does not change x.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. The hints guide the main steps."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/CurveNavigator/L05_TurningOnTheCircle.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Compute a unit tangent and curvature at a circle point.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Vector algebra; single-variable differentiation and integration. Earlier levels of Curve Navigator.\n"
"\n"
"On a regular curve, divide velocity by speed to obtain a unit tangent. Curvature is |v×a|/|v|³. At parameter zero on the unit circle, velocity is (0,1,0), acceleration is (-1,0,0), and speed is 1. Name these ingredients, then substitute them into the two geometric formulas.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `partial_div_const`
#. §1: `partial_neg`
#: Game/Levels/VectorFieldObservatory/L04_Curl.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Compute an oriented curl from coordinate partial derivatives.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Gradients, partial derivatives, and three-dimensional vectors. Earlier levels of Vector Field Observatory.\n"
"\n"
"Curl uses the right-handed difference of cross-coordinate partials. For (-y/2,x/2,0), the only nonzero component is ∂(x/2)/∂x minus ∂(-y/2)/∂y. The rules §0 and §1 express the familiar constant-factor and sign laws. Compute components without exposing coordinate updates.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/DoubleIntegralValley/L04_AreaAndVolume.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Compute area and the volume below a constant roof.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Single-variable integration; planar regions; mass and moments. Earlier levels of Double Integral Valley.\n"
"\n"
"Integrating 1 gives the rectangle area; integrating the nonnegative height 4 gives the volume below a constant roof. Calculate the inner constant integral and then the outer one in each case. The same bounds describe the two applications.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `matrix_eq`
#: Game/Levels/GradientMountain/L04_AJacobianMatrix.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Compute entries of the derivative matrix of a vector map.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Partial derivatives; unit directions; chain rule and matrices. Earlier levels of Gradient Mountain.\n"
"\n"
"The Jacobian entry in row i and column j is the j partial of output component i. For the identity map, matching coordinates give 1 and the other entries give 0. The proved rule §0 asks for the nine entries in row order, so no matrix-extensionality tactic is needed.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `cubeFlux`
#: Game/Levels/GaussCitadel/L03_FluxByBoundaryPieces.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Compute flux directly on the six outward-oriented faces.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Divergence; oriented surface flux; triple integrals; integral theorem special cases. Earlier levels of Gauss Citadel.\n"
"\n"
"Calculate outward flux directly through all six cube faces for the radial field. Each face at coordinate 1 contributes 1; each face at coordinate 0 contributes 0. Name the six actual surface integrals before adding them in §0. This calculation does not use the divergence theorem.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/DoubleIntegralValley/L05_ALaminaCentroid.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Compute mass and a normalized first moment.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Single-variable integration; planar regions; mass and moments. Earlier levels of Double Integral Valley.\n"
"\n"
"The uniform rectangle [0,2]×[0,1] has positive mass 2. Its centroid coordinates are the first moments divided by that mass. For the x moment, x is constant during the y integration. For the y moment, first integrate y over [0,1]. Keep these two different inner calculations separate.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/TripleIntegralCaverns/L04_VariableDensity.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Compute mass from a nonconstant density.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Double integrals; solid bounds; density and moments. Earlier levels of Triple Integral Caverns.\n"
"\n"
"The density x² is nonnegative on the unit cube and varies with x. Treat it as constant while integrating over y and z. The section integral remains x²; only then apply the power integration rule in x. Vanishing density on one face does not make the total mass zero.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/TripleIntegralCaverns/L06_BossTheCenterOfASolid.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Compute positive mass and three normalized moments.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Double integrals; solid bounds; density and moments. Earlier levels of Triple Integral Caverns.\n"
"\n"
"The uniform unit cube is symmetric, suggesting center (1/2,1/2,1/2). Justify that prediction by computing positive mass 1 and all three coordinate moments. Each moment requires choosing the correct varying variable. Divide by the computed positive mass, rather than using symmetry as an unproved conclusion.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. Combine earlier methods and justify every hypothesis."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `normal_components`
#. §1: `normal_congr`
#: Game/Levels/SurfaceExpedition/L03_AreaFactor.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Compute the Euclidean surface-area density.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Cross products; partial derivatives; double integration. Earlier levels of Surface Expedition.\n"
"\n"
"The surface-area factor is the Euclidean length of the parameter normal. The general rule §0 computes that normal from the two parameter derivatives. For this patch its value is (0,0,1), so its length is 1. Scalar area uses the length, while flux retains the orientation vector. A named coordinate formula is passed to §1 to replace the surface before computing its derivative columns.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `velocity_components`
#. §1: `deriv_pow_field`
#: Game/Levels/CurveNavigator/L02_DifferentiateACurve.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Compute the component derivatives of a vector curve.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Vector algebra; single-variable differentiation and integration. Earlier levels of Curve Navigator.\n"
"\n"
"Velocity differentiates the three scalar component functions. The proved rule §0 displays those three derivatives. §1 is the ordinary power rule; Lean already knows the derivatives of the identity and constant functions. Apply these rules coordinate by coordinate.\n"
"\n"
"Worked example: For c(t)=(t³,0,0), the component power rule gives velocity (3t²,0,0). Each output coordinate is differentiated separately.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. The hints guide the main steps."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/StokesSummit/L03_CurlThroughAPatch.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Compute the curl flux integrand in Stokes’ formula.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Curl; positively oriented boundaries; parametrized surface flux. Earlier levels of Stokes Summit.\n"
"\n"
"Stokes uses curl F dotted with the parameter normal. Compute the normal and the cross-coordinate derivatives for F=(0,x,0). Its curl points upward, so the dot product is 1. Keeping the normal vector rather than only its length preserves the orientation sign.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `partialX f x y`
#. §1: `deriv (fun t => f t y) x`
#. §2: `deriv_add_const`
#: Game/Levels/PartialDerivativeLab/L01_HoldYFixed.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Compute the first x partial derivative.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Joint continuity; scalar derivatives; planes and normals.\n"
"\n"
"§0 is §1: vary x while holding y fixed. Rewrite that definition, remove the constant y² with §2, and apply the power rule. A partial derivative here is a scalar rate, distinct from the total derivative linear map.\n"
"\n"
"Worked example: For f(x,y)=x³+y, holding y fixed gives the x partial 3x². The added y is constant in this derivative.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. The hints guide the main steps."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `ring`
#. §1: `ring`
#: Game/Levels/PartialDerivativeLab/L02_HoldXFixed.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Compute the first y partial derivative.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Joint continuity; scalar derivatives; planes and normals. Earlier levels of Partial Derivative Lab.\n"
"\n"
"Now hold x fixed and vary y. The same power rule gives 2y, written here as y+y. §0 proves polynomial identities such as 2y=y+y; it does not decide which variable to differentiate. Use it after applying the calculus rules.\n"
"\n"
"Worked example: For f(x,y)=xy, holding x fixed gives the y partial x. Switching the varying coordinate changes which factor is constant.\n"
"\n"
"New tools unlocked here:\n"
"\n"
"§1: Check polynomial identities, including the tangent-plane approximation error.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. The hints guide the main steps."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/GreensGarden/L03_TheInteriorIntegrand.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Compute the matching planar curl integral.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Planar line integrals; double integrals; positive orientation. Earlier levels of Green’s Garden.\n"
"\n"
"For the same field, the planar curl integrand is ∂Q/∂x-∂P/∂y=1. Calculate this derivative expression, then integrate it over the square. Its value agrees with the independent edge calculation, verifying the interior side of this example.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `surfaceNormal`
#: Game/Levels/SurfaceExpedition/L02_TangentsAndNormal.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Compute the two parameter tangents and their oriented cross product.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Cross products; partial derivatives; double integration. Earlier levels of Surface Expedition.\n"
"\n"
"Differentiate the surface while holding v fixed, then while holding u fixed. These are the two tangent vectors. Their cross product in the stated order is the oriented normal, including the area factor. Name both tangent calculations and reuse them in §0.\n"
"\n"
"Worked example: For s(u,v)=(2u,v,0), parameter tangents are (2,0,0) and (0,1,0), so the oriented area vector is (0,0,2).\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. The hints guide the main steps."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `![a,b,c]`
#. §1: `ℝ`
#. §2: `Vec`
#. §3: `rfl`
#. §4: `(![5,6,7] : Vec) 0 = 5`
#. §5: `rfl`
#: Game/Levels/Vectors/L01_ZeroAdd.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Construct a vector and extract its components.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Real arithmetic; vectors in three-dimensional space.\n"
"\n"
"§0 is a spatial vector, with coordinates numbered 0, 1, and 2. Here index 1 selects the middle entry. The type §1 means real numbers; §2 keeps the same three-coordinate representation throughout the game. Lean can read a specified coordinate directly, so the familiar §3 finishes this first calculation.\n"
"\n"
"Worked example: §4 follows by §5: index 0 selects the first entry.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. The hints guide the main steps."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/PartialDerivativeLab/L05_ATangentApproximation.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Construct the affine approximation and bound its exact error.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Joint continuity; scalar derivatives; planes and normals. Earlier levels of Partial Derivative Lab.\n"
"\n"
"At (1,1), the linear approximation to x²+y² is 2+2h+2k. Subtract it from the exact value at (1+h,1+k). The remaining h²+k² measures the approximation error and has no constant or linear terms. The polynomial tool introduced earlier now verifies this identity.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `divergence_diagonal`
#: Game/Levels/GaussCitadel/L05_RecoverAMissingFace.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Deduce an unknown flux from the closed-boundary total.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Divergence; oriented surface flux; triple integrals; integral theorem special cases. Earlier levels of Gauss Citadel.\n"
"\n"
"The five known outward face fluxes sum to 5. Find the missing top flux by first using the unit-cube Gauss theorem to calculate total flux. The general matching-partial rule §0 gives divergence 2+3+4=9. Rewrite the balance equation and solve the remaining linear equation. The balance assumption states the geometric decomposition, not the requested missing value.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/CoordinatePortal/L02_PolarAreaFactor.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Derive the polar determinant and integrate the unit disk area density.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Multiple integrals; determinants; trigonometric identities. Earlier levels of Coordinate Portal.\n"
"\n"
"Build the polar Jacobian from the actual partial derivatives of r cos θ and r sin θ, then use the 2×2 determinant rule. The sine-cosine identity simplifies the determinant to r. The assumption r≥0 permits replacing |r| by r. Finally integrate that radial area factor over the standard disk parameter domain. This evaluates the coordinate-side integral, not a general substitution theorem.\n"
"\n"
"Worked example: For T(u,v)=(3u,2v), compute derivative columns (3,0) and (0,2). Their 2×2 determinant is 6, so the area factor is |6|=6.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. The hints guide the main steps."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/StokesSummit/L01_TheBoundaryOfAPatch.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Determine all four oriented boundary traces of a parametrized patch.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Curl; positively oriented boundaries; parametrized surface flux.\n"
"\n"
"The four sides of the parameter square give the patch boundary. Traverse them with u increasing on the bottom, v increasing on the right, u decreasing on the top, and v decreasing on the left. Verify all four traces; these are the oriented edge curves used in the square circulation.\n"
"\n"
"Worked example: For a stretched rectangle s(u,v)=(2u,v,0), fixing v=0 gives its bottom edge (2u,0,0). Fix a parameter to obtain each edge.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. The hints guide the main steps."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `simp only [rules]`
#: Game/Levels/PartialDerivativeLab/L03_SecondDerivatives.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Differentiate an already computed first partial.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Joint continuity; scalar derivatives; planes and normals. Earlier levels of Partial Derivative Lab.\n"
"\n"
"A second partial differentiates an already computed first partial. For x²+y² the first x partial is 2x. Differentiating it again in x gives 2, while differentiating it in y gives 0. §0 applies just the displayed derivative rules, including under the inner function; this is the explicit-rule form of the simplification tool already learned.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `PuncturedLimit`
#. §1: `PathLimit`
#. §2: `rw [not_exists]`
#: Game/Levels/MultivariableLandscapes/L05_TwoPathsDisagree.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Disprove a common limit by uniqueness along two paths.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Domains, level sets, single-variable limits and continuity. Earlier levels of Multivariable Landscapes.\n"
"\n"
"For f=xy/(x²+y²), the horizontal and diagonal paths approach the punctured origin. §0 and §1 keep those punctured limits precise. Prove each path has a constant value away from zero. A hypothetical multivariable limit must restrict to both paths; uniqueness would force its value to be both 0 and 1/2. §2 rewrites the claim as: every proposed limit fails. You can then introduce an arbitrary limit value and its claimed property.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/CoordinatePortal/L03_CylindricalVolume.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Evaluate a correctly weighted cylindrical-coordinate integral.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Multiple integrals; determinants; trigonometric identities. Earlier levels of Coordinate Portal.\n"
"\n"
"For a unit-radius cylinder of height 2, the cylindrical volume factor is r on 0≤r≤1 and 0≤θ≤2π. Integrate the vertical coordinate first to get 2r, then the radial coordinate to get 1, then the angle. The factor is the nonnegative absolute Jacobian on this domain. The formal goal is the coordinate-side volume integral.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `LimitAtOrigin f L`
#. §1: `limit_of_continuous`
#: Game/Levels/MultivariableLandscapes/L04_ALimitFromContinuity.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Evaluate a multivariable limit using continuity.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Domains, level sets, single-variable limits and continuity. Earlier levels of Multivariable Landscapes.\n"
"\n"
"§0 is a readable name for the actual full neighborhood limit of f at the origin. The proved theorem §1 asks for continuity and the value f(0)=L. Reuse the continuity method and calculate the value separately. This is the standard continuity method for evaluating a multivariable limit.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `fun t => ...`
#: Game/Levels/CurveNavigator/L01_EndpointsOfACurve.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Evaluate a polynomial curve at its parameter endpoints.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Vector algebra; single-variable differentiation and integration.\n"
"\n"
"§0 describes a function of the parameter t. A curve assigns a spatial point to each t in its parameter interval. Here the endpoints come from substituting 0 and 1. Use the same component-comparison method as for vector addition.\n"
"\n"
"Worked example: For c(t)=(t,2t,1) on [0,1], substitute t=0 and t=1 to find (0,0,1) and (1,2,1).\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. The hints guide the main steps."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `scalarLineIntegral`
#: Game/Levels/PathIntegralTrails/L02_AWeightedWire.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Evaluate a scalar line integral with arc-length weight.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Regular parametrized curves; vector fields; the fundamental theorem of calculus. Earlier levels of Path Integral Trails.\n"
"\n"
"A scalar line integral multiplies the scalar density by speed. For this unit-speed wire, the density along the path is t and the speed is 1. Prove the resulting integrand first, replace it in §0, then perform the ordinary integral. Arc-length weight is always nonnegative on the increasing parameter interval.\n"
"\n"
"Worked example: A constant scalar density 1 on a path of speed 2, for 0≤t≤3, gives scalar line integral ∫₀³2 dt=6.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. The hints guide the main steps."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/TripleIntegralCaverns/L01_ARectangularBox.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Evaluate a triple iterated integral on a box.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Double integrals; solid bounds; density and moments.\n"
"\n"
"Evaluate a triple integral from the innermost variable outward. The z integral is 4; integrating that value over the y interval gives 12; the final x interval doubles it. The box has ordered bounds and continuous constant density. This is the three-variable version of the rectangle method.\n"
"\n"
"Worked example: A box with side lengths 2, 2, and 3 has constant-density integral 3, then 6, then 12 when integrated from the innermost variable outward.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. The hints guide the main steps."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/DoubleIntegralValley/L01_ARectangle.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Evaluate an iterated integral on a rectangle.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Single-variable integration; planar regions; mass and moments.\n"
"\n"
"In an iterated integral, evaluate the innermost variable first. Here integrating 1 with respect to y on [0,3] gives 3. Name that result, substitute it into the x integral, and integrate the remaining constant on [0,2]. All bounds are ordered and the integrands are continuous. The result is the rectangle area.\n"
"\n"
"Worked example: On [0,4]×[0,2], first ∫₀²1 dy=2, then ∫₀⁴2 dx=8. Name the inner value and rewrite it in the outer integral.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. The hints guide the main steps."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/VectorFieldObservatory/L01_CompareTwoFields.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Evaluate and distinguish vector fields at a point.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Gradients, partial derivatives, and three-dimensional vectors.\n"
"\n"
"A vector field assigns a vector to each position. The radial field returns the position itself, while the rotational field rotates and scales its planar components. Evaluate both at the same point to compare their behaviour. Coordinate calculations now use only familiar vector methods.\n"
"\n"
"Worked example: For F(x,y,z)=(2x,y,0), substituting (1,3,0) gives (2,3,0). A field evaluation takes a position as input and returns a vector.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. The hints guide the main steps."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `squareCirculation`
#: Game/Levels/GreensGarden/L02_CirculationByEdges.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Evaluate circulation directly along a closed boundary.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Planar line integrals; double integrals; positive orientation. Earlier levels of Green’s Garden.\n"
"\n"
"Compute circulation directly by evaluating work on each of the four counterclockwise edges. Only the right edge contributes for F=(0,x,0). Name the four edge values before adding them with §0. This supplies an independent boundary calculation before any use of Green’s theorem.\n"
"\n"
"Worked example: For F=(0,2x,0), the upward right edge x=1 has work integrand 2, giving contribution 2 over its unit parameter interval.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. The hints guide the main steps."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/StokesSummit/L04_TheBoundaryCalculation.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Evaluate circulation with the orientation induced by the surface.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Curl; positively oriented boundaries; parametrized surface flux. Earlier levels of Stokes Summit.\n"
"\n"
"Evaluate boundary circulation directly for F=(0,2x,0). Use the same four oriented edges as in Green’s Garden. Only the upward right edge contributes, with value 2. This independently checks the boundary side before invoking the surface theorem.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `rw [dot]`
#: Game/Levels/Vectors/L03_TheDotProduct.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Evaluate the coordinate formula for an inner product.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Real arithmetic; vectors in three-dimensional space. Earlier levels of Vector Workshop.\n"
"\n"
"The dot product multiplies matching components and adds the results. §0 opens that coordinate formula, just as rewriting opened definitions in the Natural Number Game. Then use the numerical arithmetic tool from the preceding level.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/SpaceExplorer/L04_DistanceToAPlane.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Evaluate the normal-distance formula with a nonzero normal.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Vector Workshop; parametric lines, normals, and quadratic equations. Earlier levels of Space Explorer.\n"
"\n"
"Distance from q to a plane is |n·(q-p)| divided by the Euclidean length of its nonzero normal. For the plane z=1, choose n=(0,0,1), whose length is 1. Absolute value makes this a nonnegative distance, independent of the side of the plane.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/CoordinatePortal/L04_SphericalVolume.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Evaluate the spherical volume density on its standard domain.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Multiple integrals; determinants; trigonometric identities. Earlier levels of Coordinate Portal.\n"
"\n"
"On 0≤r≤1, 0≤φ≤π, 0≤θ≤2π, the spherical factor is r² sin φ. First justify the absolute-value sign using nonnegativity of a square and of sine on [0,π]. Then perform the radial, polar-angle, and azimuth integrations in that order. This is a proved coordinate-integral calculation; identification with a Cartesian ball by a general substitution theorem remains deferred.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/OptimizationArena/L03_SaddleWitnesses.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Exhibit both upward and downward directions arbitrarily near a saddle.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: First and second partial derivatives; compact regions; elementary inequalities. Earlier levels of Optimization Arena.\n"
"\n"
"For x²-y², the displacements (t,0) and (0,t) give t² and -t². For every nonzero t they lie on opposite sides of the critical value. Since t may be arbitrarily small, these witnesses explain the saddle behaviour. Begin by establishing the sign of t² once and reusing it.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/TripleIntegralCaverns/L02_BoundsForASolid.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Express a sloping-roof solid with explicit bounds.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Double integrals; solid bounds; density and moments. Earlier levels of Triple Integral Caverns.\n"
"\n"
"The solid has roof z=x, with 0≤z≤x≤1 and 0≤y≤1. Translate between the expanded bounds and the shorter nested description. As with triangular regions, x≥0 follows from 0≤z≤x. Read and assemble the inequalities explicitly; the task is choosing valid bounds for a solid.\n"
"\n"
"Worked example: For a roof z≤y with z≥0, the inequality y≥0 follows. A solid description must also state both bounds on the independent horizontal coordinate.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. The hints guide the main steps."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `∧`
#: Game/Levels/MultivariableLandscapes/L01_AConstrainedDomain.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Express and verify a domain with explicit inequalities.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Domains, level sets, single-variable limits and continuity.\n"
"\n"
"For √(4-x²-y²)/(x-y), a real-valued geometric domain requires x²+y²≤4 and x-y≠0. At (1,0), these are two concrete checks. §0 collects the independent restrictions. Lean defines division and square root on all real inputs, so recording the intended domain is part of the mathematics.\n"
"\n"
"Worked example: For √(9-x²-y²)/(x+y), the point (2,1) meets 2²+1²≤9 and 2+1≠0. Both domain checks are necessary.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. The hints guide the main steps."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/SpaceExplorer/L03_LineMeetsPlane.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Find a point satisfying both geometric constraints.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Vector Workshop; parametric lines, normals, and quadratic equations. Earlier levels of Space Explorer.\n"
"\n"
"A line-plane intersection must satisfy two geometric conditions. The vertical line reaches height 3 at parameter 3. Supply that witness for line membership, then independently check the plane equation. This is an application of existence, vector comparison, and dot products.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `h.1`
#. §1: `h.2`
#: Game/Levels/OptimizationArena/L01_ACriticalPoint.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Find all critical points of a quadratic function.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: First and second partial derivatives; compact regions; elementary inequalities.\n"
"\n"
"Find every critical point of x²+y² by setting both first partials equal to zero. The notation §0 and §1 retrieves the first and second facts of a conjunction h, just as reading components retrieves coordinates of a vector. Solve both directions: stationarity forces the origin, and the origin satisfies stationarity.\n"
"\n"
"Worked example: For f=(x-1)²+y², setting the two first partials to zero locates (1,0). Locating a critical point does not yet classify it.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. The hints guide the main steps."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/GaussCitadel/L02_DivergenceInTheSolid.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Integrate a field divergence over the cube.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Divergence; oriented surface flux; triple integrals; integral theorem special cases. Earlier levels of Gauss Citadel.\n"
"\n"
"For the smooth radial field, the three matching coordinate partials give divergence 3. Establish that formula at an arbitrary point before evaluating its triple integral. The unit cube has unit volume, so integrating this constant gives 3. This is the volume side of the divergence identity.\n"
"\n"
"Worked example: For F=(2x,2y,2z), divergence is 6. On a unit cube its volume integral is 6 times volume 1.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. The hints guide the main steps."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/SurfaceExpedition/L04_AScalarSurfaceIntegral.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Integrate a scalar density with the area factor.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Cross products; partial derivatives; double integration. Earlier levels of Surface Expedition.\n"
"\n"
"A scalar surface integral multiplies the scalar density at the surface point by the area factor. Here the density is u and the factor is 1. Name the normal, evaluate the inner integral as u, and integrate it over [0,1]. The increasing parameter bounds give nonnegative area weighting.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/SurfaceExpedition/L05_OrientedFlux.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Integrate a vector field against an oriented area vector.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Cross products; partial derivatives; double integration. Earlier levels of Surface Expedition.\n"
"\n"
"Flux of the upward unit field through this upward-oriented patch uses the dot product with the unnormalized parameter normal. Its integrand is 1. Calculate that oriented integrand before integrating; no additional area-factor multiplication is needed.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/PathIntegralTrails/L03_WorkAlongACurve.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Integrate a vector field dotted with the curve velocity.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Regular parametrized curves; vector fields; the fundamental theorem of calculus. Earlier levels of Path Integral Trails.\n"
"\n"
"Work integrates F(c(t))·c′(t). Substitute the path into the field, compute velocity, and take the dot product before integrating. This integrand has an orientation-sensitive velocity, unlike the speed factor in a scalar line integral.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/TripleIntegralCaverns/L05_AFirstMoment.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Integrate position times density.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Double integrals; solid bounds; density and moments. Earlier levels of Triple Integral Caverns.\n"
"\n"
"The x first moment multiplies density x² by position x. After the y and z integrations, use algebra to express the section as x³, then apply the power integral. This distinguishes mass from the moment used in a center-of-mass numerator.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `integral_values`
#. §1: `constant_integral`
#: Game/Levels/CurveNavigator/L04_ArcLengthOfASegment.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Integrate the speed of a regular curve on ordered bounds.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Vector algebra; single-variable differentiation and integration. Earlier levels of Curve Navigator.\n"
"\n"
"For increasing bounds, arc length integrates the nonnegative speed. This segment is regular and has constant speed 1. Prove that speed formula first. §0 replaces an integrand by a pointwise equal function, and §1 integrates a constant as interval length times value.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `normal_graph`
#: Game/Levels/StokesSummit/L06_BossChooseASimplerSurface.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Justify replacing a graph by a planar patch with the same oriented boundary.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Curl; positively oriented boundaries; parametrized surface flux. Earlier levels of Stokes Summit.\n"
"\n"
"The raised polynomial graph has exactly the same four oriented boundary traces as the flat patch. Prove those trace equalities first. The graph-normal rule §0 has vertical component 1, and this field has vertical unit curl, so both flux integrands equal 1. Together with the proved flat-patch Stokes family, this justifies the simpler-surface calculation in this concrete case; it does not assume a general surface-independence theorem.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. Combine earlier methods and justify every hypothesis."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `simp`
#: Game/Levels/PathIntegralTrails/L01_ChooseAnOrientation.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Parametrize a line segment with specified initial and final points.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Regular parametrized curves; vector fields; the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n"
"\n"
"The segment (1-t)A+tB runs from A at parameter 0 to B at parameter 1. Its order gives the path orientation. Verify the endpoint identities using scalar-multiplication identities; §0 removes the zero and unit coefficients.\n"
"\n"
"Worked example: The segment from (0,0,0) to (2,0,0) has midpoint (1,0,0) at t=1/2, start at t=0, and finish at t=1.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. The hints guide the main steps."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/OptimizationArena/L06_BossAGlobalConstrainedOptimum.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Prove a feasible point attains a global optimum.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: First and second partial derivatives; compact regions; elementary inequalities. Earlier levels of Optimization Arena.\n"
"\n"
"Verify the proposed optimizer (1,1) lies on the constraint and has value 2. For an arbitrary feasible point, the identity (x+y)²+(x-y)²=2(x²+y²) and a nonnegative square bound the objective by 2. This proves a global maximum, not merely a stationary candidate. Use the square-bound method from Gradient Mountain.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. Combine earlier methods and justify every hypothesis."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `ascent_squares`
#. §1: `le_of_sq_le_sq`
#: Game/Levels/GradientMountain/L05_TheAscentBound.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Prove a sharp bound for a gradient dotted with a unit direction.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Partial derivatives; unit directions; chain rule and matrices. Earlier levels of Gradient Mountain.\n"
"\n"
"For gradient (3,4,0), the unit-direction rate cannot exceed 5. The supplied algebraic identity §0 decomposes 25|u|² into the squared rate and two nonnegative terms. Use the unit assumption and nonnegativity to bound the squared rate, then §1 with nonnegative upper bound 5. You choose and combine the estimates.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `continuous_fill_origin`
#: Game/Levels/MultivariableLandscapes/L06_BossRepairTheOrigin.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Prove continuity of a separately defined extension at the origin.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Domains, level sets, single-variable limits and continuity. Earlier levels of Multivariable Landscapes.\n"
"\n"
"Filling in an exceptional point preserves continuity only when its assigned value matches the continuous extension. Apply §0: you must prove that the polynomial is continuous and that its value at the origin is exactly the proposed replacement 0. The support rule handles the equality of the piecewise function, not these mathematical checks.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. Combine earlier methods and justify every hypothesis."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `Continuous f`
#. §1: `continuous_sum_squares`
#. §2: `continuous_apply 0`
#. §3: `1`
#. §4: `2`
#: Game/Levels/MultivariableLandscapes/L03_PolynomialContinuity.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Prove joint continuity of a polynomial in three variables.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Domains, level sets, single-variable limits and continuity. Earlier levels of Multivariable Landscapes.\n"
"\n"
"§0 means joint continuity in all coordinates. The rule §1 says that squaring and adding three continuous scalar functions preserves continuity. Apply it and supply continuity of each coordinate projection with §2, §3, and §4. No filter manipulation is needed.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `simp`
#. §1: `eq_comm`
#. §2: `simp`
#: Game/Levels/SpaceExplorer/L05_AParaboloidAndItsTraces.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Recognize an elliptic paraboloid and compute two traces.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Vector Workshop; parametric lines, normals, and quadratic equations. Earlier levels of Space Explorer.\n"
"\n"
"The surface z=x²+y² is an elliptic paraboloid. Setting y=0 gives a parabola; setting z=1 gives a circle. §0 removes routine identities such as 0²=0. It is useful after choosing the mathematical substitution. §1 states that an equality can be read in either direction.\n"
"\n"
"New tools unlocked here:\n"
"\n"
"§2: Simplify using elementary identities and the explicitly supplied definitions.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/GreensGarden/L06_BossAreaFromTheBoundary.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Recover enclosed area from a boundary circulation.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Planar line integrals; double integrals; positive orientation. Earlier levels of Green’s Garden.\n"
"\n"
"The area formula uses the boundary field (-y/2,x/2,0), whose planar curl is 1. Compute the boundary integral directly on the four positively oriented edges, then compare with the double integral of 1. The two nonzero edge contributions are each 1/2. This proves the stated area identity for the unit square.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. Combine earlier methods and justify every hypothesis."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/GradientMountain/L06_BossTangentToALevelSet.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Relate a level-set equation, its gradient, and tangent directions.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Partial derivatives; unit directions; chain rule and matrices. Earlier levels of Gradient Mountain.\n"
"\n"
"For f(p)=p₀, the level surface f=2 is a plane. A tangent displacement has first coordinate zero, so it preserves the level equation. Compute the gradient and verify its dot product with the tangent direction is zero. This links an equation, a derivative, and geometry without introducing new proof tools.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. Combine earlier methods and justify every hypothesis."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `work`
#. §1: `reverse_integral`
#: Game/Levels/PathIntegralTrails/L04_ReverseOrientation.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Show that reversing oriented integration negates work.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Regular parametrized curves; vector fields; the fundamental theorem of calculus. Earlier levels of Path Integral Trails.\n"
"\n"
"Reversing the parameter bounds reverses oriented work. Open the two occurrences of §0 and apply the interval-reversal rule §1. This statement concerns the oriented integral; it does not negate a geometric arc length.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `mul_left_cancel₀`
#. §1: `left`
#. §2: `right`
#. §3: `mul_eq_zero.mp factor`
#: Game/Levels/OptimizationArena/L05_LagrangeEquations.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Solve the Lagrange multiplier system and find both candidates.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: First and second partial derivatives; compact regions; elementary inequalities. Earlier levels of Optimization Arena.\n"
"\n"
"For x+y on x²+y²=2, the stationarity equations give 1=λ(2x) and 1=λ(2y). First show λ is nonzero, then cancel it using §0. The constraint gives x²=1 after x=y, and factoring produces the two signs. §1 and §2 select the candidate established in each branch. These stationary candidates still require a global comparison. §3 uses the forward direction of the product-zero equivalence to obtain the two factor alternatives.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `onLine p v q`
#. §1: `use 2`
#: Game/Levels/SpaceExplorer/L01_APointOnALine.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Supply a parameter witnessing line membership.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Vector Workshop; parametric lines, normals, and quadratic equations.\n"
"\n"
"§0 says that q=p+t v for some real parameter t. As in an existence proof in NNG, §1 supplies a witness. Then compare the three coordinates with the vector rule from Vector Workshop. A correct parameter must satisfy every coordinate.\n"
"\n"
"Worked example: The line p=(0,0,0)+t(1,0,0) contains (3,0,0), witnessed by t=3.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. The hints guide the main steps."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `use`
#: Game/Levels/VectorFieldObservatory/L06_BossConstructAPotential.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Supply a potential and verify it globally.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Gradients, partial derivatives, and three-dimensional vectors. Earlier levels of Vector Field Observatory.\n"
"\n"
"Construct a globally defined potential for (y,x,0) and normalize its value to zero at the origin. Supply the scalar function with §0, check its value, then calculate its gradient at an arbitrary point. Reuse the product partial rule rather than inferring conservativity from curl alone.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. Combine earlier methods and justify every hypothesis."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `.1`
#. §1: `.2`
#: Game/Levels/DoubleIntegralValley/L02_ATriangularRegion.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Translate a triangular region into equivalent bounds.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Single-variable integration; planar regions; mass and moments. Earlier levels of Double Integral Valley.\n"
"\n"
"For the triangle 0≤y≤x≤1, the lower bound x≥0 follows from the other inequalities. Prove the two descriptions equivalent. For nested conjunctions, §0 reads the first condition and §1 reads the remaining conditions; repeat these selectors to reach later bounds. No new calculus tactic is involved.\n"
"\n"
"Worked example: For 0≤x≤y≤2, one may omit the separate y≥0 bound because 0≤x and x≤y imply it by transitivity.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. The hints guide the main steps."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `work_first_coordinate`
#: Game/Levels/PathIntegralTrails/L06_BossPathIndependence.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Use a potential to compare two paths and compute their common work.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Regular parametrized curves; vector fields; the fundamental theorem of calculus. Earlier levels of Path Integral Trails.\n"
"\n"
"For potential φ=x, the endpoint rule §0 is proved from the fundamental theorem with the stated path regularity and derivative-integrability hypotheses. Apply it to each path. Their shared start and finish then force equal work, and the first equality gives its value as final x minus initial x. This proves path independence for the specified conservative field.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. Combine earlier methods and justify every hypothesis."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `gradient_components`
#. §1: `partial_sum_squares`
#: Game/Levels/PartialDerivativeLab/L06_BossAnImplicitTangentPlane.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Use a regular gradient to identify a tangent plane.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Joint continuity; scalar derivatives; planes and normals. Earlier levels of Partial Derivative Lab.\n"
"\n"
"For the unit sphere at (1,0,0), verify the point lies on the surface, calculate its gradient, prove that gradient is nonzero, and identify its point-normal tangent plane. §0 displays the three partials; §1 supplies the squared-coordinate power rule. Regularity is the nonzero-gradient check, which must not be omitted.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. Combine earlier methods and justify every hypothesis."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `RegularPath`
#. §1: `PotentialAlong`
#. §2: `WorkIntegrable`
#: Game/Levels/PathIntegralTrails/L05_ApplyTheEndpointTheorem.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Use an established fundamental theorem for line integrals.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Regular parametrized curves; vector fields; the fundamental theorem of calculus. Earlier levels of Path Integral Trails.\n"
"\n"
"§0 records differentiability of every coordinate along the interval. §1 states the derivative of the potential along the curve equals F·c′, and §2 records integrability of that actual integrand. These names abbreviate the established derivative and integral conditions; none assumes the desired work equality. Apply the endpoint theorem and supply its two analytic hypotheses.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/CoordinatePortal/L06_BossAnAbsoluteJacobian.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Verify a change-of-variables equality with an orientation-reversing map.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Multiple integrals; determinants; trigonometric identities. Earlier levels of Coordinate Portal.\n"
"\n"
"The orientation-reversing map sends the unit square to [0,2]×[-3,0]. Compare the original constant-density integral with the transformed one using the absolute determinant 6. Calculate the rectangle integral and determinant independently, then combine them. This verifies the stated change-of-variables special case by direct integration.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. Combine earlier methods and justify every hypothesis."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `↔`
#. §1: `constructor`
#. §2: `intro h`
#. §3: `linarith`
#. §4: `linarith`
#: Game/Levels/SpaceExplorer/L02_APlaneFromANormal.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Verify a plane equation from its point-normal definition.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Vector Workshop; parametric lines, normals, and quadratic equations. Earlier levels of Space Explorer.\n"
"\n"
"A point q lies in the plane through p with normal n when n·(q-p)=0. The normal (0,0,2) is nonzero. The symbol §0 asks for both directions of an equivalence: use §1, then §2 in each direction. After expanding the plane equation, §3 combines linear real equations; here it turns 2(q₂-3)=0 into q₂=3.\n"
"\n"
"Worked example: With normal (0,0,1) and base height 5, the point-normal equation is q₂-5=0, equivalent to q₂=5. A scaled nonzero normal describes the same plane.\n"
"\n"
"New tools unlocked here:\n"
"\n"
"§4: Solve linear equations or inequalities arising from a plane equation.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. The hints guide the main steps."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `curl_congr`
#. §1: `divergence_congr`
#: Game/Levels/VectorFieldObservatory/L05_TwoDifferentialIdentities.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Verify curl-of-gradient and divergence-of-curl for explicit smooth fields.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Gradients, partial derivatives, and three-dimensional vectors. Earlier levels of Vector Field Observatory.\n"
"\n"
"First compute the constant gradient of x and the constant curl of the rotational field. The rules §0 and §1 allow replacement of equal fields. Derivatives of constant components then give zero. These are explicit smooth examples of the differential identities, not an assumption that mixed partials commute without regularity.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `squareCirculation`
#: Game/Levels/GreensGarden/L01_APositiveBoundary.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Verify endpoints of counterclockwise square edges.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Planar line integrals; double integrals; positive orientation.\n"
"\n"
"A positive planar boundary goes counterclockwise, keeping its region on the left. The square edges run right along the bottom, up the right, left along the top, and down the left. Check that the first two joins match. Their explicit parameter directions determine the orientation used by §0.\n"
"\n"
"Worked example: A circle traversed counterclockwise has its disk on the left. Positive square edges obey the same left-of-motion convention.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. The hints guide the main steps."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/PartialDerivativeLab/L04_MixedPartialsAgree.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Verify mixed partial equality for an explicitly smooth polynomial.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Joint continuity; scalar derivatives; planes and normals. Earlier levels of Partial Derivative Lab.\n"
"\n"
"For the smooth polynomial xy, the first partials are y and x. Differentiating in the other variable gives 1 in both orders. This calculation proves equality for this smooth example, without assuming a general interchange theorem. Identify the fixed factor before choosing the multiplication rule.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `cubeFlux`
#: Game/Levels/GaussCitadel/L01_AnOutwardClosedBoundary.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Verify outward orientation on all six cube faces.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Divergence; oriented surface flux; triple integrals; integral theorem special cases.\n"
"\n"
"A closed cube has six faces, and every face must point outward. The parameter order differs between opposite faces. Calculate all six cross-product normals: +x, -x, +y, -y, +z, -z. The same six parametrizations occur in §0, so the closed-boundary orientation is fully explicit.\n"
"\n"
"Worked example: On the top plane z=2 of a box, the parameter order (s,t,2) produces an upward normal, away from its interior. Opposite faces require opposite normals.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. The hints guide the main steps."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/GreensGarden/L05_GreenInFluxForm.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Verify outward planar flux equals the divergence integral.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Planar line integrals; double integrals; positive orientation. Earlier levels of Green’s Garden.\n"
"\n"
"For F=(x,y,0), compute outward planar flux using the four specified unit conormals, then compute the integral of divergence 2. Right and top edges contribute 1 each; bottom and left contribute zero. Equality of these independent calculations verifies the flux-form Green special case, with outward conormals distinct from boundary tangent directions.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `integral_values`
#: Game/Levels/DoubleIntegralValley/L03_ReverseTheOrder.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Verify reversed bounds by evaluating both integrals.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Single-variable integration; planar regions; mass and moments. Earlier levels of Double Integral Valley.\n"
"\n"
"The same triangle has bounds 0≤y≤x≤1 or 0≤y≤1 with y≤x≤1. For density 1, compute the inner integrals as x and 1-y. §0 substitutes these pointwise formulas under the outer integrals. Splitting an integral of a difference requires integrability; continuity of 1 and y on this bounded interval supplies it. This is a directly verified order-reversal special case.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `partial_xy`
#: Game/Levels/VectorFieldObservatory/L02_RecognizeAGradient.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Verify that a field has a specified potential.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Gradients, partial derivatives, and three-dimensional vectors. Earlier levels of Vector Field Observatory.\n"
"\n"
"For the globally defined scalar potential φ=xy, calculate its gradient as (y,x,0). Use the product partial rule §0 with the component gradient formula. Producing an actual potential proves this field is conservative on all of space; no curl-free domain shortcut is needed.\n"
"\n"
"Worked example: For φ=xz, holding the other coordinates fixed gives gradient (z,0,x). A proposed potential is verified by all three partial derivatives.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. The hints guide the main steps."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `patch u v`
#: Game/Levels/SurfaceExpedition/L01_ParametrizeASurface.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Verify that a patch lies in a specified surface.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Cross products; partial derivatives; double integration.\n"
"\n"
"A surface parametrization has two independent parameters. §0 maps (u,v) to (u,v,0), placing every point in the horizontal plane. Read its height and two planar coordinates directly. The parameter rectangle used later fixes the actual bounded patch.\n"
"\n"
"Worked example: For s(u,v)=(u,v,u+v), substituting (1,2) gives (1,2,3). A surface uses two parameter inputs.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. The hints guide the main steps."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `squarePotential`
#: Game/Levels/MultivariableLandscapes/L02_ALevelSet.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Verify that a point lies on a specified level surface.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Domains, level sets, single-variable limits and continuity. Earlier levels of Multivariable Landscapes.\n"
"\n"
"A level set consists of points where a scalar function has one fixed value. For x²+y²+z²=9, substitute the proposed point into §0. This verifies membership in the sphere, rather than a bound on its interior.\n"
"\n"
"Worked example: At (2,0,0), the sum-of-squares field has value 4, so that point belongs to its level set f=4.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. The hints guide the main steps."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/StokesSummit/L02_MatchOrientations.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Verify the normal and oriented top boundary agree.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Curl; positively oriented boundaries; parametrized surface flux. Earlier levels of Stokes Summit.\n"
"\n"
"The upward parameter normal matches a counterclockwise boundary by the right-hand rule. Along the top edge, the tangent must point in the negative x direction. Verify both the surface normal and this oriented boundary tangent; matching a set of boundary points alone would not determine orientation.\n"
"\n"
"Worked example: A downward normal matches clockwise boundary motion when viewed from above. Reversing both orientations preserves the Stokes sign relation.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. The hints guide the main steps."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/CoordinatePortal/L01_PolarIdentities.lean:13
msgid "Objective: Verify the radial identity for polar coordinates.\n"
"\n"
"Mathematical prerequisites: Multiple integrals; determinants; trigonometric identities.\n"
"\n"
"Polar coordinates use x=r cos θ and y=r sin θ. Factor out r² and use sin²θ+cos²θ=1. The identity holds for all real r and θ; using polar coordinates as a single-cover region additionally requires r≥0 and an angular interval of length 2π, with the usual exceptional origin and boundary.\n"
"\n"
"Worked example: When r=2, (2 cos θ)²+(2 sin θ)²=4(sin²θ+cos²θ)=4. The variable-radius proof uses the same factorization.\n"
"\n"
"Exercise: prove the statement below. The hints guide the main steps."
msgstr ""

